==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|discention|dissention||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{inh+|en|enm|dissencioun}}, from {{der|en|frm|dissention}}, {{m|frm|discention}}, from {{der|en|fro|dissension}}, from {{der|en|la|dissensio}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsɛnʃən/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|dis|sen|sion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An act of expressing [[dissent]], especially spoken.
# Strong disagreement; a contention or quarrel; discord.
#* {{RQ:Elyot Governour|year=1907|chapter=The Best Governance|page=11|passage={{...}}wherby at the last shuld have [[sourd|sourded]] '''discention''' amonge the people, they beinge seperately enclined towarde theyr naturall souerayne lorde{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1843|author=E. A. Poe|title=s:Morning on the Wissahiccon
|passage=The natural scenery of America has often been contrasted, in its general features as well as in detail, with the landscape of the Old World—more especially of Europe—and not deeper has been the enthusiasm, than wide the '''dissension''', of the supporters of each region.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Deborah J. Bennett|title=Randomness|publisher=Harvard University Press|page=34f
|passage=In Biblical times the resort to chance was an agreed-upon way of making many decisions because it ended '''dissension''' among opposing, often powerful, parties.}}

